SECTION I.
Introduction

Partially automated driving, Level 2 of the SAE International [1] , is meanwhile state of the art in the premium segment of vehicle manufacturers. At this level of driving automation, the automated driving system (ADS) of the automated vehicle (AV) completely takes over the longitudinal and lateral vehicle guidance [1] . The driver is no longer actively involved in the primary driving task. Nevertheless, the driver is responsible for monitoring the AV, its system status and driving behavior at all times. This is necessary since the driver must be able to take over the vehicle guidance immediately in case of a request to intervene due to a detected system limit of the AV. A sufficient time budget (e.g., 5–7 seconds) as with higher automation levels is not feasible with partially automated driving [2] . An even greater challenge are malfunctions compared to system limits with a request to intervene, because these failures are not detected by ADS. The driver must be able to determine on his or her own whether the vehicle is in control of the situation or not and intervene if necessary [3] . Malfunctions have been identified as a high safety risk in previous studies [4] , [5] . Twenty-seven percent of reported accidents due to malfunctions were getting off the road [5] and there was a 28% crash rate [4] . In complex urban areas, the controllability of such malfunctions by the driver could be even worse. The complexity of urban areas is a result of a large number of static and dynamic objects [6] , high information density and the large number of different types of road users [7] . Situations that are more complex can prolong the decision making time [8] . This may be problematic, since time-critical situations can arise in urban areas [9] . It is therefore important to investigate the effects of malfunctions in partially automated urban driving and how the driver can be supported during such malfunction scenarios.
A. Relevance of an Automation Human-Machine Interface

In order to support the driver in safely handling malfunctions during partially automated driving, it is essential to provide adequate information to the driver via a so-called automation Human-Machine Interface (HMI) [10] . Here, information about the system status and the maneuvers of the AV is considered important [11] –​ [13] . In addition, information about maneuver reasons, environmental information, speed and speed limits also seems to be important in partially automated driving [14] . This has also been confirmed for urban automated driving [15] . The design of the HMI is challenging; the driver should be adequately informed by the HMI, but at the same time the driver should not be visually distracted in the monitoring task of the driving environment [16] , since more than 90% of information is perceived visually while driving [17] . It has been observed that partially automated driving leads to reduced visual attention and longer off-road glances compared to manual driving [18] . Since a low level of visual attention to the forward driving environment and long off-road glances (longer than 2–3 seconds) during automated driving are associated with a high crash risk [19] , one main goal of the HMI should be to guarantee a sufficient level of attention [20] . The HMI should further contribute to a reduction of stress through its design [20] , since parallel monitoring of the driving environment and the HMI may cause high stress and workload levels [21] . Furthermore, acceptance of the AV in combination with the HMI should be achieved, otherwise drivers intend to not use the ADS [22] . Without sufficient trust, this requirement for acceptance cannot be fulfilled [23] . An increase in trust can be achieved by helping the user to understand the automation’s decisions [24] . However, it is important to not only prevent mistrust, but also to not create over-trust. For instance, drivers showed over-trust if they were not appropriately supported in their monitoring responsibilities [25] . The design of the HMI should therefore guarantee correct trust calibration [20] . By fulfilling the usability requirement, the HMI should support the driver effectively, efficiently and satisfactorily [26] during a take-over caused by malfunctions and in monitoring the partially ADS.
B. Potential of Augmented Reality Head-Up Displays

Augmented Reality Head-Up Displays (ARHUDs) represent an innovative approach to the design of the HMI. The first investigations in the automotive context were already carried out in the 1970s [27] , but are now experiencing an increase in relevance due to their technical feasibility. An ARHUD is characterized by displaying virtual objects and information in the driving environment in real time [28] . This allows the information to be displayed at the actual position and thus virtual objects can overlay the environment from the drivers’ perspective [29] . ARHUD can thus direct the drivers’ attention via the virtual objects to allow them to react faster [30] without having to take their eyes off the road. Therefore, ARHUDs may lead to higher safety and comfort [31] . Previous research in the context of automated driving has so far shown positive subjective results [32] –​ [36] and higher visual attention to the driving environment when using an ARHUD [32] , [37] . There is also a positive trend for the ARHUD in regard to take-over performance, but the results depend on the scenarios and the design of the ARHUD and are less clear [37] , [38] . A pre-study [32] showed decreased subjective workload and increased trust of an ARHUD concept compared to a conventional head-up display (HUD) concept for monitoring in partially automated urban driving. Additionally, the ARHUD resulted in higher visual attention to the driving environment [32] . In regard to the monitoring of functionalities of an adaptive cruise control, drivers reported a higher level of safety and a lower level of workload and distraction when using an ARHUD compared to an instrument cluster [33] . Showing drivers an augmented visualization of the vehicle’s sensor data also improved acceptance and trust in the AV [34] . In a further study [35] , participants reported that they would prefer an ARHUD compared to an instrument cluster in conditionally automated driving. They considered the ARHUD more useful and easier to understand [35] . It could also be demonstrated that ARHUDs can improve situation awareness in automated driving [36] . Regarding automation failures in automated driving, an ARHUD led to shorter reaction times of the driver [38] . In contrast, another study [37] showed no differences in take-over time or crash rate when comparing an ARHUD to an instrument cluster. However, it found a higher visual attention ratio to the driving environment [37] .
SECTION II.
Objectives

In order to comply with the challenges of partially automated driving in complex urban areas from a human-centered perspective, an integrated HMI seems to be of great importance. With regard to malfunctions in partially automated driving, ARHUDs could have a high potential. It has not yet been investigated if an ARHUD actually provides better support for the driver in case of malfunctions during partially automated driving in urban areas. For this purpose, an ARHUD concept has been developed, which should be compared to a Baseline concept consisting of only an instrument cluster and an LED strip (see Section III ). In a driving simulation experiment, the two concepts were evaluated regarding their effects on take-over time, take-over quality, visual attention and subjective ratings. This was to investigate the following research questions (RQs). Due to the positive subjective results of ARHUDs in previous research (see Section I . B .), we expected the ARHUD to result in higher subjective ratings than the Baseline concept.

    RQ1:

    Does take-over time and quality differ depending on the HMI concept?
    RQ2:

    Are there differences in take-over time and quality between the lateral and longitudinal malfunction?
    RQ3:

    Does visual attention differ depending on the HMI concept?
    RQ4:

    Does the ARHUD concept lead to higher subjective ratings compared to the Baseline concept?

On an explorative level, we wanted to additionally investigate whether safety and controllability can be ensured for the investigated malfunction scenarios.
SECTION III.
HMI Design
A. Baseline Concept

The Baseline concept consisted of a freely programmable instrument cluster and a LED strip mounted at the bottom of the windshield. The instrument cluster design was based on two previously conducted studies [39] , [40] . It displayed several driving- and automation-related information items such as the system status of the ADS, current speed, speed limits, navigation, as well as actual and planned maneuvers. Since the system status was considered the most relevant information for automated driving [14] , [15] , this information was additionally displayed via an LED strip. The LED strip is particularly beneficial due to its good peripheral perceptibility [10] and has already been used in several studies on automated driving (e.g., [15] , [39] , [41] –​ [43] ). The active ADS was indicated by a blue illumination of the LED strip, since the color blue is peripherally well perceptible [44] and was rated suitable for this use case [41] . Furthermore, the color blue was proposed for LED strips in the context of partially automated driving [39] , [45] . Fig. 1 shows the blue LED strip and the instrument cluster in the vehicle interior during the partially active ADS. In the event of a request to intervene, a take-over icon was displayed in the instrument cluster, the LED flashed red, a warning tone appeared and the seat vibrated. The participants thus received multimodal warnings to take over vehicle guidance again. Although the participants experienced system limits with the mentioned design of the request to intervene during the familiarization drive, this had no relevance to the investigated malfunction scenarios, since no requests to intervene occurred during these scenarios.
