2) Development:

The ARHUD concept consisted of the Baseline concept, but was extended by an additional ARHUD ( Fig. 2 ). The ARHUD concept was based on a pre-study [32] and was further developed. We displayed the driving-relevant information regarding AV guidance in the ARHUD. In addition to the augmented reality elements, the current speed was continously displayed in a conventional HUD. If an augmented visualization of the navigation information was not yet possible, e.g., because the intersection was not completely visible yet, this information was additionally displayed situation-specific in a two-dimensional manner in the HUD [50] . When the ADS was active, a single boomerang or boomerang chain was displayed in front of the AV ( Fig. 2 ). When the vehicle was in free driving mode or stopped and no explicit maneuver such as turning at a intersection or a lane change was present, a single boomerang was displayed in front of the vehicle to reduce the risk of concealing other objects [50] ( Fig. 2.a ). The boomerang had the width of the AV, since this is the basis for estimating the lateral deviation of the AV [52] . The boomerang was displayed at a distance of 12 m in front of the AV, which was about 0.9 seconds away at 50 km/h and thus at the lower limit of the driver’s fixations [53] , [54] . The speed of 50 km/h was relevant because of the speed of 50 km/h set during the malfunction scenarios. In case of a lane change or a turning maneuver, the single boomerang was extended to a boomerang chain, which displayed the planned trajectory for an enhanced system understanding ( Fig. 2.b and Fig. 2.c ). Additionally, six more boomerang elements with a gap of 1 m in front of the single boomerang were shown. When following a vehicle in front, the system switched to the adaptive cruise control (ACC) mode. The distance to the vehicle in front was visualized with a variable number of boomerang elements depending on the distance, which showed the control range as known from conventional ACC icons ( Fig. 2.d ). To indicate that the vehicle in front was detected, it was marked by a green bracket at the bottom [51] . Static and dynamic obstacles in the AV’s lane were marked with a round yellow bracket ( Fig. 2.c ). This round bracket should additionally mark the lateral distance to the obstacle when passing it. A rectangular bracket marked traffic lights, oncoming traffic, as well as traffic signs that had an influence on driving strategy, such as “Speed limit” or “Give way” ( Fig. 2.a ). The rectangular brackets marking traffic lights had the color of the state of the traffic light.
