SECTION IV.
Method
A. Sample

The participants were recruited via postings at the Technical University of Munich. The prerequisite for participation was the possession of a valid driver’s license for at least three years. Fifty-two participants took part in the driving simulator experiment. Due to simulator sickness, three participants had to abort the experiment. One participant had to be excluded because of nonfulfillment of the prerequisites for participation. This resulted in a total of 48 valid data sets. The mean age of the ARHUD group was M = 25.8 years ( SD = 7.3) and the mean age of the Baseline group was M = 24.9 years ( SD = 2.2). The ARHUD and the Baseline group each consisted of 10 (41.7%) women and 14 (58.3%) men. One participant in the Baseline and two participants in the ARHUD condition had a red-green color vision deficiency. When asked how regularly they drove in the past year, in the Baseline condition one participant reported daily, five participants reported several times a week, nine participants reported weekly, and nine participants reported monthly. In contrast, in the ARHUD condition, two participants reported driving daily, five participants reported driving several times a week, seven participants reported driving weekly, and 10 participants reported driving monthly. In the ARHUD group, 45.8% and in the Baseline group, 50.0% already had experience with a partially ADS. Both groups reported a high knowledge of automated driving ( Mdn = 4.0) rated on a 5-point Likert scale from “very low (1)” to “very high (5)”.
B. Experimental Design

We implemented a 2 × 2 mixed-subject design for the driving simulator study. The HMI concept (ARHUD or Baseline) was a between-subject factor and the malfunction scenario (longitudinal or lateral) was a within-subject factor. This was to avoid participants experiencing the same malfunction scenario multiple times. The malfunction scenarios were presented to the participants in permutated order. The experimental drive was interrupted after each take-over situation so that the participants did not have to drive for more than 10 minutes without a break. This should reduce vigilance decrements while the participants had to monitor the ADS [12] , [57] . Participants were instructed that it was not necessary to keep their hands on the steering wheel when the ADS was active, since no reduced crash outcomes or improved driver conflict response were found when driving hands-on [4] , [58] , [59] . Additional non-driving related activities (NDRAs) in partially automated driving result in a higher number and longer duration of off-road glances [18] . A potential resulting low visual attention is associated with an increased crash probability [19] , [60] . Furthermore, performing NDRA during automated driving caused more lane excursions and longer take-over times [61] . To avoid such a negative influence of the NDRA, the participants were not allowed to engage in NDRAs during this study.

The ethics committee of the Technical University of Munich reviewed and approved this study. The ethical approval code is 533/19 S.
C. Dependent Variables

We collected objective and subjective data during the experiment. We recorded take-over time, maximum lateral acceleration, minimum longitudinal acceleration and minimum time-to-collision (mTTC) [62] to evaluate take-over quality at system limits [63] . The crash rate was also recorded. Causing no crash was defined as pass-criteria against which controllability can be assessed [64] . According to ISO 26262 standard [65] , controllability is achieved if 90.0% of the participants fulfill this pass-criterion (class of controllability: C2). Visual attention was operationalized by the attention ratio on the areas of interest (AOIs) Road Ahead (corresponding to the windscreen) and Instrument Cluster , as well as off-road glances with a duration of more than 2 seconds [60] . Mirrors were not considered as further AOI, since these showed values of less than one percent in a pre-study [32] and should not be affected by the HMI concepts. Off-road glances greater than 2 seconds are considered to be an increased crash risk [60] . Using standardized questionnaires, we measured usability [66] , subjective workload [67] , [68] , trust [69] and acceptance [70] . Additionally, participants rated the malfunction scenarios regarding the time budget (from 1 = “not sufficient at all” to 5 = “completely sufficient”), criticality (from 1 = “very critical” to 5 = “very uncritical”), complexity (from 1 = “very complex” to 5 = “very simple”) and predictability (from 1 = “very unpredictable” to 5 = “very predictable”) directly following the encountered malfunction scenario. These ratings were based on the factors classifying take-over scenarios [71] . The questionnaires were provided in German. Table III shows a list of the investigated dependent variables and their description.
