D. Apparatus

We conducted the experiment in the static driving simulator of the Chair of Ergonomics at the Technical University of Munich [72] ( Fig. 3 ). Three front projectors generated a 180° front view and three rear projectors generated the side and rear mirrors. Additionally, the front center projector realized the ARHUD visualization. The driving simulator software SILAB 6.0 of the Würzburg Institute for Traffic Sciences [73] had a refresh rate of 60 Hz for visualization and a data collection rate of 240 Hz. A sound system created engine and environmental sounds. We recorded the eye-tracking data using the remote eye-tracking system SmartEye [74] using three stationary infrared cameras in the vehicle interior running at 60 Hz. The ADS could be activated by pressing a button on the steering wheel. The ADS could be deactivated at any time by the participants by pressing the same button, by pressing the brake or accelerator pedal or by a steering intervention.
