E. Experimental Track and Design of Malfunction Scenarios

The experimental track was a 27-minute automated drive, consisting of typical urban driving scenarios, such as following a vehicle in front, passing road bottlenecks or turning at intersections with traffic lights. After 7 minutes of automated driving, a system limit scenario with a request to intervene occurred. The results of this scenario are not part of this publication. This was followed by two parts of 10 minutes automated driving followed by the lateral or longitudinal malfunction scenario depending on the permutation.
1) Longitudinal Malfunction Scenario:

Before the malfunction occurred, the AV was on the right lane of a two-lane road and followed a van with a time gap of 2 seconds at a speed of 50 km/h (see Fig. 2.d ). The van then changed to the left lane and a stopped vehicle became visible in the right lane (see Fig. 4.a ). The ADS failed to recognize the new vehicle and drove towards the stopped vehicle without any request to intervene. The participants then had a time budget of 2.6 seconds to intervene in order to prevent a collision with the stopped vehicle. The scenario was based on cut-out scenarios in partially automated driving showing similar time budgets of 2.3-3 seconds on highways and rural roads [58] , [59] . According to [71] , we classified this scenario as having high urgency, low predictability, high criticality and high complexity of the necessary driver response. In the event of the malfunction, the ARHUD changed from ACC mode to free driving mode due to the missed detection of the stopped vehicle. This was also displayed accordingly in the instrument cluster.
