2) Lateral Malfunction Scenario:

The failure of the lateral control took place during a long left-hand bend. At a speed of 50 km/h, the AV malfunctioned in planning the right trajectory and drifted straight towards a car parked on the right-hand hard shoulder (see Fig. 4.b ). From the time of leaving the ideal trajectory, the participants had a time budget of 3.7 seconds to regain control of the vehicle and avoid a collision with the parked car. The deviation from the ideal trajectory was difficult to perceive in the beginning, so that we extended the time budget by approximately one second compared to the longitudinal malfunction. This scenario is based on [5] , [75] , [76] , investigating similar malfunction and system limit scenarios for partially automated driving on highways. We classified this scenario as having high urgency, low predictability, high criticality and medium complexity of the necessary driver response [71] . During this scenario, the ARHUD displayed the malfunctioning trajectory heading towards the parked car via the single boomerang. Meanwhile, the instrument cluster presented the free driving mode.
F. Procedure

The duration of the experiment was 75 minutes. After the welcoming, participants had to read and sign the safety instruction and declaration of consent. Afterwards, participants had to fill out the demographic questionnaire. Participants were instructed in written form for reasons of standardization about the driving simulator, the capabilities of the partially ADS, the possibility of malfunctions and, depending on the ARHUD or Baseline condition, about the HMI concept. After entering the vehicle and adjusting the mirrors and seat, an 8-minute familiarization drive took place. During the familiarization drive, participants had to drive manually and then experienced an automated drive with two request to intervene scenarios by using the Baseline concept. A malfunction did not occur during the familiarization drive. Then the eye-tracking system was calibrated and the 27-minute experimental drive was started. Each take-over during the experimental drive was followed by an intermediate survey. After the experimental drive, a follow-up questionnaire was conducted. The experiment was concluded with payment of a compensation of 10 ¬.
G. Statistical Analysis

Data preparation and visualization was done with Matlab and MS Excel. We used JASP 0.12 [77] for the statistical analysis. We checked the normality distribution and equality of variances by using the Shapiro–Wilk test and Levene’s test. If these assumptions for parametric tests were not met, we used the non-parametric alternative. We conducted an ANOVA independently of the assumption of normal distribution, since this seems to be robust against a violation of normal distribution [78] . Single items on Likert-scales were classified as ordinal data [79] . All other data were considered as interval scaled. A significance level of α = 0.05 was applied. In case of multiple comparisons, a Bonferroni-Holm correction [80] was performed and the resulting α H o l m was reported in the test statistics. We interpreted the effect sizes based on Cohen’s benchmark (small: d = 0.20, r = 0.10, partial η 2 = 0.01; medium: d = 0.50, r = 0.30 partial η 2 = 0.06; large: d = 0.80, r = 0.50 partial η 2 = 0.14) [81] . Due to crashes or a lack of eye-tracking quality, which led to exclusions of participants regarding the take-over quality or visual attention, the sample sizes for all objective metrics are reported in the following.
SECTION V.
Results
A. Driving Data

The take-over time is visualized in Fig. 5 . The corresponding descriptive statistics are given in Table IV . All participants took over vehicle guidance during the malfunction scenario, so that none of them caused a crash without an intervention. A mixed ANOVA revealed a significant effect on take-over time for the HMI concept ( F (1,46) = 7.173, p = 0.010, partial η 2 = 0.135) and for the malfunction scenario ( F (1,46) = 687.590, p < 0.001 , partial η 2 = 0.937). There was no significant interaction effect between the HMI concept and the malfunction scenario ( F (1,46) = 2.278, p = 0.138). An exploratory analysis showed that 24 participants in the Baseline and 12 participants in the ARHUD group had a time budget of 0.90 seconds or less for collision avoidance in the lateral malfunction at the time they took over vehicle guidance. For the longitudinal malfunction, eight participants in the Baseline and five participants in the ARHUD group had 0.90 seconds or less for collision avoidance at the time when they took over vehicle guidance.
