During the longitudinal malfunction, 41.7% of the Baseline group and 25.0% of the ARHUD group crashed. Three participants did not cause a crash with the stopped vehicle, but came off the road and collided with a street lamp. For the lateral malfunction, the crash rates reached 83.3% for the Baseline group and 37.5% for the ARHUD group. It was found that the ARHUD leads to considerably fewer crashes than the Baseline. Furthermore, the lateral malfunction led to more crashes than the longitudinal malfunction independent of the HMI concept. Since the collision object (parked vehicle) was not located in the lane of the AV in case of the lateral malfunction, we could not determine the mTTC due to missing recorded data; we therefore listed the mTTC only for the longitudinal malfunction ( Table IV ). We found no significant effect between the Baseline and ARHUD for the mTTC ( t (30) = 0.229, p = 0.820). Since we had to exclude participants that caused a crash from the analysis of the minimum longitudinal and lateral acceleration, this resulted in a very small sample size for the lateral malfunction scenario. Therefore, no statistical analysis of accelerations was possible in this scenario. Neither was it possible to compare the two scenarios in terms of acceleration. We only considered the accelerations during the lateral malfunction on a descriptive level. Since there were violations of the assumption of the normality distribution for the longitudinal acceleration and the lateral acceleration during the longitudinal malfunction, we conducted Mann-Whitney tests in these cases. No significant effect was revealed for longitudinal acceleration ( Table IV ) during the longitudinal malfunction ( U = 135.0, p = 0.750) between both HMI concepts. Although longitudinal acceleration for the lateral malfunction descriptively shows a lower mean value for the ARHUD compared to the Baseline, no real evaluation can be made because of the small sample size. The lateral acceleration ( Table IV ) showed no significant effect for the longitudinal malfunction ( U = 120.0, p = 0.837) comparing the Baseline and ARHUD. On a descriptive level, lateral acceleration showed also no difference for the lateral malfunction.
B. Eye-Tracking Data

The eye-tracking data of one participant was missing due to technical problems. Additionally, the eye-tracking data of two participants were excluded due to a data availability of less than 70.0% for both 10-minute automated drives before the malfunction [82] . This resulted in 23 valid datasets of the ARHUD group and 22 valid datasets of the Baseline group for the eye-tracking data. The remaining datasets had a mean data availability of 94.0% for the Baseline and of 92.9% for the ARHUD which is classified as good [82] . Fig. 6 shows the boxplots of the attention ratio on the AOIs Road Ahead and Instrument Cluster . The ARHUD resulted in a mean attention ratio of M = 89.9% ( SD = 5.0%) on the Road Ahead and in an M = 1.7% ( SD = 1.2%) on the Instrument Cluster . In comparison, the Baseline resulted in an M = 88.7% ( SD = 3.3%) on the Road Ahead and in an M = 4.0% ( SD = 2.6%) on the Instrument Cluster . The data of the attention ratio were not normally distributed and additionally, the assumption for equal variances were not fulfilled for the Instrument Cluster . There was no significant difference for the Road Ahead ( U = 321.0, p = 0.126, α H o l m = 0.010). In contrast, the Instrument Cluster showed a significant lower attention ratio for the ARHUD compared to the Baseline ( U = 100.0, p < 0.001 , r = 0.605, α H o l m = 0.008).
