SECTION VI.
Discussion and Limitations
A. Take-Over Time and Quality

The statistical analysis showed a significantly shorter take-over time for ARHUD. Considering the take-over time for both malfunction scenarios separately, it seems that this is mainly due to the faster take-over time of the ARHUD during lateral malfunction. Even though there was only a difference of 0.21 seconds on the average take-over time between ARHUD and Baseline, we nevertheless assume that even this difference might be essential for the reduction of the crash rate. Emergency brake assistants initiate an intervention at a time budget of 0.90 seconds [6] , since this is the last point in time at which the collision can be avoided by braking only. Therefore, we believe it makes a difference whether participants have a remaining average time budget of 0.88 seconds in the ARHUD compared 0.67 seconds in the Baseline condition to avoid a collision after they took over vehicle guidance in the lateral malfunction. On a descriptive level, both HMI concepts have a similar impact on the take-over time during longitudinal malfunction. The participants could detect that the AV is in danger of frontal collision with the stopped vehicle independently of the HMI concept. In this case, the ARHUD does not seem to provide faster intervention. In contrast, during lateral malfunction, the participants may be able to anticipate the trajectory of the AV better due to the more transparent visualization in the driving environment and thus to recognize the necessary intervention more quickly. This would be consistent with the literature stating that an ARHUD resulted in significantly faster response time in the case of a lateral malfunction (lane change failure) [38] , whereas no faster response time was detected in the case of a longitudinal malfunction (potential crash with front vehicle) [37] . Consequently, the scenario seems to have a considerable influence on the extent to which ARHUDs lead to a faster take-over time. On average, the ARHUD resulted in a take-over time of 2.8 seconds for the lateral malfunction with a time budget of 3.7 seconds, so that a time budget of 0.9 seconds remained to prevent the collision. These 0.9 seconds correspond to the moment when the displayed boomerang of the ARHUD reached the collision object, in this case the parked vehicle. Thus, there could be a great potential to display the single boomerang not 0.9 seconds in front of the vehicle but probably at the upper limit of 2 seconds [54] . There was a significant difference in the take-over time between both malfunction scenarios. In the longitudinal malfunction scenario, participants took over the vehicle guidance significantly faster. This could be caused by the fact that the takeover time increases with an increasing time budget [63] . This contradicts that the participants perceived no difference in the time budget of the two malfunction scenarios according to the rating of the scenario characteristics. We assume that it is more likely to be due to the slow deviation of the middle of the lane becoming clearly visible at a later point in time and therefore resulting in longer take-over times during the lateral malfunction scenario.

When using the ARHUD, considerably fewer crashes occurred in both malfunction scenarios. This difference in the crash rate by the ARHUD is greater for the lateral malfunction than for the longitudinal malfunction. This could be due to the longer take-over times of the ARHUD during the lateral malfunction, since crashing drivers react slower than non-crashing drivers during the response process due to a malfunction [58] . Additionally, long braking reaction times lead to a higher crash probability [83] . In general, the investigated lateral malfunction seems to be less controllable than the longitudinal malfunction. The very high crash rate compared to previous studies [4] , [5] , [37] could be in consequence of the urban driving environment and the resulting high information density. An investigated urban malfunction during automated driving in a multi-vehicle simulation also showed a higher crash rate of 40% [39] . Thus, our investigated scenarios showed a higher time criticality compared to these studies. With regard to the other dependent variables of take-over quality, no differences were found between the HMI concepts. A comparison of the scenarios regarding these dependent variables was not possible. This was due to the small sample size, since participants with a collision were excluded from the analysis of the mTTC, minimum longitudinal acceleration and maximum lateral acceleration. Due to the reduced sample size, effects regarding the take-over quality might have not been detected. Although the ARHUD seems to provide significant support in terms of take-over time and quality, no controllability according to ISO 26262 [65] was demonstrated for the investigated malfunction scenarios as a result of the high crash rate for either HMI concept. This demonstrates the need for a fallback level for partially automated urban driving. Although partially automated driving according to the SAE International J3016 [1] does not take into account non-sustained functions such as emergency braking [84] , such functions may already contribute to a significant crash reduction if the driver fails to intervene adequately. The number of vehicles equipped with an automatic emergency brake assistant is already rapidly increasing [85] . Nevertheless, it is important that the emergency brake assistant is a redundant system so that, for example, if a sensor failure occurs that leads to a malfunction of the partially ADS, this failure does not also affect the emergency brake assistant.
B. Visual Attention

There were no differences in the attention ratio on the Road Ahead between the two HMI concepts. Both HMI concepts resulted in high visual attention to the Road Ahead , so we assume that inattention should not have a strong effect on the take-over performance. With a high proportion of eyes on the road, the prerequisite for being able to visually detect malfunctions at all seems to be fulfilled. However, it is apparently not enough to just have eyes on the road to realize that the vehicle cannot handle the situation and an intervention is necessary [4] . Based on the results of the take-over time and quality, the ARHUD seems to better indicate the need for intervention to the driver. Another factor that could have a negative impact on take-over despite high visual attention would be mind-wandering. During mind-wandering, participants shift their mental attention to spend time with their own thoughts, which could lead to longer braking response times [86] . The high attention ratios to the Road Ahead compared to [18] could be due to experiencing two take-over situations with a request to intervene during the familiarization drive or differences in the instruction. With regard to the attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster , the Baseline concept showed a significantly higher ratio. Since there was no difference in the number of off-road glances longer than 2 seconds between the two HMI concepts, it can be concluded that the higher attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster for the Baseline does not cause negative effects in the form of long off-road glances compared to the ARHUD. Nevertheless, even glances of normal duration can cause crashes if the timing is bad [87] , e.g., during the occurrence of the malfunction. Therefore, the ARHUD seems to be beneficial, since a lower attention ratio on the Instrument Cluster was necessary. The low absolute number of long off-road glances should nevertheless only be compared relatively between the two concepts. If participants were looking towards the vehicle interior and thus not on the AOIs Instrument Cluster or Road Ahead , the positioning of the three stationary eye-tracking cameras may have led to non-available data and off-road glances were not recorded as such.
C. Subjective Ratings

The ARHUD led to a significantly higher usability compared to the Baseline. The usability of the ARHUD can be classified as good and that of the Baseline as OK [88] . Consequently, the participants felt that the ARHUD was more effective, more efficient and more satisfactory in terms of handling malfunctions and for monitoring in partially automated driving. With regard to the acceptance rating, there were no significant differences between the two HMI concepts, so that it cannot be clearly concluded which of the systems the participants are more likely to use. However, the ARHUD seems to result in higher acceptance on a descriptive level. The ARHUD resulted in a significantly higher trust in automation, which could confirm the results of [32] , [34] . In any case, a crash during a take-over could reduce the trust level in the AV [89] . However, since the descriptive analysis of the trust scores in the ARHUD condition showed no decrease in trust because of a crash, it can be assumed that the increased trust in the ARHUD condition indeed is due to the design of the ARHUD. Whereas the pre-study by [32] showed a significantly lower workload when using an ARHUD to monitor the partially ADS, this could not be confirmed for the investigated malfunction scenarios. There was no significant difference in workload between the HMI concepts or between the scenarios. Reference [21] listed mean workload levels of automated driving studies, ranging from 10 to 42 for the NASA-TLX. Our results indicate higher workloads regardless of the HMI concept and scenario. From this, it could be concluded that the malfunctions were generally perceived as demanding in the context of automated driving. The ratings of the malfunction characteristics by the participants showed high criticality, relatively low predictability, medium complexity of the driver response and low to medium time budget. There were no significant differences except for predictability, which was perceived less predictable for the longitudinal malfunction compared to the lateral malfunction. This could be because the longitudinal malfunction was only detectable from the moment the van changed to the left lane and the stopped vehicle became visible. In the case of the lateral malfunction, the participants might perceive slow drifting away from the middle of the lane as more predictive.
D. Further Limitations

One limitation is the young average age of the sample of this study. This could limit the generalizability of the results, since younger participants showed better results than older participants in monitoring and detecting failures of an automated system [90] . Older participants may show longer reaction times during the monitoring task [90] , which in the context of this study could have a negative effect on the take-over time and quality. The participants of this study were not used to partially automated driving in urban areas and the presented HMI concepts. Reference [91] reported that drivers needed up to three weeks to be used to an ACC system. Thus, the results may change if the participants had already been accustomed to the investigated HMI concepts or ADS of this study. Even though we used a high-fidelity driving simulator to ensure a high degree of immersion, it cannot be completely ruled out that the simulator may have created a false sense of safety [92] . This could lead to higher risk tolerance, even though we could not see any indication for over-trust or any similar phenomena. In addition, the absolute values of the dependent variables longitudinal and lateral acceleration might be limited due to the missing vehicle dynamics of the static driving simulator compared to dynamic driving simulators or real vehicles [93] . Nevertheless, we consider our used driving simulator suitable for the relative comparison of the HMI concepts [93] .
SECTION VII.
Conclusion and Future Work

The results indicate that the ARHUD supports drivers in partially automated driving better than the Baseline, resulting in shorter take-over times and fewer crashes. This effect is intensified in the case of the lateral malfunction scenario, which generally appears to be more difficult to control. Furthermore, both HMI concepts showed a high level of attention to the driving environment. Here the ARHUD could have a further advantage, since the participants had a lower attention ratio on the instrument cluster in the vehicle interior. In terms of trust in automation and usability, the ARHUD showed an increase, whereas no significant improvement was found for acceptance and workload. However, the high visual attention to the driving environment and the reduction of the take-over time by the ARHUD do not seem to be sufficient for crash avoidance, since the malfunctions were not classified as controllable in general. A fallback level for partially automated driving by a redundant system, for example an emergency brake assistant, seems to be indispensable in urban areas.

In the future, it should be investigated whether the augmented reality visualization of the trajectory at a larger distance, e.g., 2 seconds in front of the AV, could further improve the controllability by the ARHUD. Furthermore, we suggest that a fallback level should be implemented and its effects on the controllability and subjective rating of the drivers should be investigated. In our investigations we have chosen very time-critical malfunction scenarios in urban areas. To what extent the effects found can be transferred to scenarios with a larger time budget will require a separate investigation.
